hed into  something a little bit more sinister um  but really this is you know just the  nature of these videos and how quickly  they can spread on social media makes it  really difficult to say where it  originated or who might have started  these videos yeah of course and i wonder  too if there's any sort of uh legal  ramifications in this for the students  who've made these videos could they  get into any sort of legal trouble  i don't know for sure but i know that  schools have really gone above and  beyond trying to you know suss out where  these are coming from um  it's probably a big question for school  officials and lawmakers yeah and i  should mention in terms of the federal  response uh tick tock said that they are  working with law enforcement to look  into warnings about potential violence  at these schools i am curious what that  tells you about kind of where tic toc  sees their role coming in here and  specifically bike dance the parent  company of tick tock do they see  themselves as kind of  needing to cooperate a little bit more  than say other nameless social media  platforms might if they were in the same  situation  that's a good question you know we have  seen statements so far from tick tock as  well as  some federal law enforcement agencies  like the department of homeland security  as well as the fbi generally they have  been pretty  plain statements they're just urging  anybody with any information to come  forward and that they're  um  so far are not aware of any really  serious credible threats to schools  it's a really good question you know tic  toc is concerned with this kind of  trend that you know allegedly originated  on its platform they want to get to the  source of where these come from and it's  it's a big question not just for tick  tock but for snapchat where some of  these videos have also been seen  sure and also i know that in your  reporting one of your sources talked  about how  it's not just this challenge there's  a his